%% Lifecycle of ONE A record in a privatelink zone - the only supported state, the four drift states that look identical from the client, and how each one is left
%% Source: Microsoft Learn "Azure Private Endpoint DNS integration" (zone group behaviour, five zones per group, one zone per zone name, one zone group per endpoint, records deleted with the endpoint, caution on duplicate and overridden zones); codex/runbook-private-endpoint-name-resolution-and-reachability.md classes 1, 2, 3 and 6
stateDiagram-v2
  direction TB

  state "No zone - the name has never resolved privately anywhere" as Absent
  state "Zone exists and is linked, but holds no record for this account - the client gets NXDOMAIN, never a fallthrough to public DNS" as Empty
  state "Managed by the DNS zone group - the platform writes and rewrites the record as the endpoint changes. The only supported state" as Managed
  state "Hand-made A record - created with record-set a add-record because no zone group was attached" as HandMade
  state "Shadowed - a second zone of the same name is linked to the same VNet and answers first, holding no record" as Shadowed
  state "Stale - the endpoint was recreated with a new NIC address and the record still carries the old one" as Stale
  state "Overridden on one host - a hosts file entry on a single server wins over every DNS answer and is invisible to every DNS test" as HostsFile
  state "Deleted - the endpoint is gone and the zone group removed its records with it" as Deleted

  [*] --> Absent
  Absent --> Empty : create the privatelink zone and link it to the hub and the spokes
  Empty --> Managed : attach a DNS zone group on the private endpoint referencing the CENTRAL zone
  Empty --> HandMade : shortcut taken when the zone lives in another subscription and the operator lacks Private DNS Zone Contributor
  Absent --> HostsFile : the documented testing-only fallback on the Migrate appliance, kept after testing

  Managed --> Deleted : delete the private endpoint, the zone group deletes its records
  Managed --> Shadowed : Azure Migrate creates its own zone of the same name in the project resource group and someone links it to the hub
  Managed --> Stale : endpoint recreated while an intermediate resolver or client still caches the previous answer
  HandMade --> Stale : endpoint recreated, nothing rewrites a hand-made record
  Shadowed --> Managed : delete or unlink the duplicate zone, then re-verify from a Contoso server
  Stale --> Managed : fix the record through the zone group, Clear-DnsClientCache on the client, purge the Infoblox cache for that zone
  HandMade --> Managed : attach the zone group and delete the hand-made record, in that order
  HostsFile --> Managed : add the Infoblox forward zone, remove the hosts entry, retry registration
  Deleted --> Absent : release the Infoblox IPAM reservation for the address as well

  note right of Managed
    Constraints of the zone group.
    Five private DNS zones per group at most.
    One zone per zone NAME per group.
    One zone group per private endpoint - you
    cannot add a second one.
    Deleting the endpoint deletes the records.
  end note

  note right of Shadowed
    Two zones with the same name linked to one
    VNet do not merge. Microsoft's guidance is a
    single zone per name for the whole estate,
    linked to hub and spokes. Merging afterwards
    is a manual record-by-record operation.
  end note

  note right of HostsFile
    The anti-pattern that hides all of the above.
    Resolve-DnsName on that server still shows the
    public chain while the application reaches the
    private address, so the DNS evidence and the
    application behaviour disagree. Closure rule in
    the runbook - no hosts entry and no hand-made
    record survives the incident.
  end note
